{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"06a01c70ddaebba9602661605265c39fe3a9930fa61538daec197887554559fb","slug":"pages.m05-linear-relationships-6-of-8","location":"/pages/057-m05-linear-relationships-6-of-8.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Least Squares: Finding the Best-Fitting Line","content_includes_title":false,"authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"City College of San Francisco","given":"City College","non_dropping_particle":"of","family":"San Francisco"},"name":"City College of San Francisco","id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-0"}],"license":{"content":{"id":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","name":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","CC":true}},"numbering":{"title":{"offset":2}},"thumbnail":"/textbook/build/m05-candidate-lines-dab1325d60740099186f66489cd26c7a.svg","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"057-m05-linear-relationships-6-of-8.md","url":"/textbook/build/057-m05-linear-relat-d726327223407e67b07bde62e66ed53d.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Learning Objectives","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"cLdjbmxZY4"}],"key":"HTqAHzT1Ta"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In the special case of linear relationship, use the least squares regression line as a summary of the overall pattern, and use it to make predictions.","position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"key":"J9DEbJcqis"}],"key":"W7Bd6NyOcT"}],"key":"te2jMtCQL8"}],"key":"NKhUhsoHzv"}],"class":"note","key":"EQDU4KeONn"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The technique that specifies the dependence of the response variable on the explanatory variable is called ","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"dEBUZOgucR"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"regression","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"eIUJa93FUw"}],"key":"gtaAGITGfc"},{"type":"text","value":". When that dependence is linear (which is the case in our examples in this section), the technique is called ","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"b1sxx9rtb4"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"linear regression","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"IUcHiqJtET"}],"key":"Zys1eC5Fan"},{"type":"text","value":". Linear regression is therefore the technique of finding the line that best fits the pattern of the linear relationship (or in other words, the line that best describes how the response variable linearly depends on the explanatory variable).","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"dzQYCOuOEb"}],"key":"dqjxypW03h"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"To understand how such a line is chosen, consider the following very simplified version of the age-distance example (we left just 6 of the drivers on the scatterplot). There are many lines that look like they would be good candidates to be the line that best fits the data:","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"jNyDI2ac9m"}],"key":"u4zOyFPjST"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/textbook/build/m05-candidate-lines-dab1325d60740099186f66489cd26c7a.svg","alt":"A scatterplot of six data points with three different candidate lines drawn through them: a solid red line and two dashed lines with different slopes. All three look like plausible summaries of the downward linear pattern.","key":"L29VH61flA","urlSource":"images/gen/m05-candidate-lines.svg"}],"enumerator":"1","key":"lqenTJfO6y"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is doubtful that everyone would select the same line in the plot above. We need to agree on what we mean by “best fits the data”; in other words, we need to agree on a criterion by which we would select this line. We want the line we choose to be close to the data points. In other words, whatever criterion we choose, it had better somehow take into account the vertical deviations of the data points from the line, which are marked in blue in the plot below:","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"rA2AdriSIb"}],"key":"gJBqv8HPfX"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/textbook/build/m05-vertical-deviati-a8809d7ba834eb203e5cde93c5a92d69.svg","alt":"The six data points with a single red line through them. At each point a thick blue vertical segment connects the point to the line, showing the vertical deviation of the point from the line. Some points are above the line and some below.","key":"JySHdNGpBh","urlSource":"images/gen/m05-vertical-deviations.svg"}],"enumerator":"2","key":"tQzfKPIbm9"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The most commonly used criterion is called the ","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"key":"lmEiLfI7eL"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"childr